## Overview

This system serves on-demand video globally with fast startup, minimal rebuffering, and consistent “continue watching” across devices. The elegant core is to treat video delivery as **static immutable objects** (segments + manifests) served at the edge, while keeping the “dynamic” parts (entitlements, DRM licenses, session policy, resume state) in a small, highly reliable control plane.

The key insight: **optimize the hot path for cacheability**. Once you package content into cache-friendly CMAF segments and short, frequently-refreshed manifests, your scaling problem becomes “make bytes cheap” (CDN) and “make decisions correct” (auth/DRM/steering/resume). Everything else is boring pipeline engineering.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive designs underestimate two traps:
1) **ABR isn’t just encoding ladders**—it’s about segment duration, keyframe alignment, and CDN/origin behavior. Small packaging mistakes silently create rebuffer storms under load.
2) **Global delivery is a routing problem disguised as storage**. Multi-CDN placement, regional origins, and failover policies decide whether you degrade gracefully or globally crater during a provider incident.

